     "claim": "G_kayak",
     "type": "established",
     "claim_text": "On 9 August 1979 Forrest Tucker, then 59 and serving a robbery sentence at San Quentin State Prison, California, escaped with two other inmates in a homemade kayak across the bay. Narration: 'A bank robber paddled out of San Quentin.'",
     "urls": [
      "https://www.tampabay.com/archive/1999/05/01/accused-bank-robber-78-shows-no-signs-of-retiring/",
      "https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Latest-News-Wires/2013/0415/Redoine-Faid-s-brazen-escape-and-other-notorious-prison-breaks",
      "https://sanquentinnews.com/back-in-the-day-2/",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forrest_Tucker_(criminal)",
      "https://libcat.ru/knigi/starinnaya-literatura/190487-54-david-grann-the-devil-sherlock-holmes.html"
     ]
    },
    {
     "claim": "G_count",
     "type": "established",
     "claim_text": "REPORTED (his own count): Tucker said he had escaped 18 times (his manuscript outline asked whether anyone had escaped 18 times, Tampa Bay Times 1999; 'by his own reckoning ... 18 times successfully and 12 times unsuccessfully', per Wikipedia citing Grann). Grann's article: he broke out 'almost twenty times'. One lower-quality page says 16; the count is therefore attributed: 'He said he escaped 18 times', end card 'BY HIS COUNT'.",
     "urls": [
      "https://www.tampabay.com/archive/1999/05/01/accused-bank-robber-78-shows-no-signs-of-retiring/",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forrest_Tucker_(criminal)",
      "https://archive.proz.com/translation-contests/propose_source_text/436"
     ]
    }
